 L('parallel-slope','Parallel lines in coordinates','Nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes. Use slope to test or construct parallel lines.','3x-2y=8',['Rewrite: y=(3/2)x-4.','Slope=3/2.','Any parallel nonvertical line has slope 3/2.','Use a given point to determine its intercept.'],'parallel slope 3/2','Find an equation parallel to y=-2x+7 through (3,1).','y=-2x+7','Verify both the slope and the required point.','Parallelism is a direction condition encoded by equal slopes.'),
 L('perpendicular-slope','Perpendicular lines in coordinates','For nonvertical lines, perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals, so their product is -1.','3x-2y=8; perpendicular through (6,1)',['Original slope=3/2.','Perpendicular slope=-2/3.','y-1=(-2/3)(x-6).','y=(-2/3)x+5.'],'y=(-2/3)x+5','Write a line perpendicular to y=(1/4)x-2 through (4,3).','y=-4x+19','Check slope product -1 and substitute the point.','Negative reciprocal slopes reflect a 90-degree direction change.'),
 L('converse','Proving lines parallel','Converse angle theorems work backward: if the required angle relationship is known, you can conclude the lines are parallel.','A pair of alternate interior angles are congruent',['Identify them as alternate interior angles.','Use the converse of the alternate interior angles theorem.','Conclude the lines are parallel.'],'lines are parallel','If corresponding angles measure 72° and 72°, what can you conclude about the two lines?','They are parallel.','Name the converse theorem that justifies the conclusion.','Converse theorems convert observed angle relationships into evidence of parallelism.'),
 L('mixed','Coordinate and angle reasoning','Choose between angle theorems and slope criteria based on the form of the evidence. Do not force an algebraic slope method onto a diagram or vice versa.','Two lines have slopes 2/5 and -5/2',['Their product is -1.','They are perpendicular.','No angle measurement is needed.'],'perpendicular','Lines have slopes -3 and -3. What relationship is possible, and what else must you check to know if they are distinct?','parallel or same line; compare intercepts/points','Name the criterion and any additional information needed.','A complete conclusion uses the right criterion and recognizes cases where one feature is not enough.')
