 L('rotation','Rotations','A rotation turns every point around a center by the same angle. Around the origin, standard coordinate rules make quarter-turns efficient.','Rotate (3,1) 90° counterclockwise about origin',['Rule: (x,y)->(-y,x).','(3,1)->(-1,3).','Distance from origin stays sqrt(10).'],'(-1,3)','Rotate (-2,5) 180° about the origin.','(2,-5)','Name the center, angle, direction, and coordinate rule.','Rotations preserve distance from the center and all rigid-motion properties.'),
 L('dilation','Dilations','A dilation multiplies distances from a center by a scale factor. Angles are preserved, but lengths change unless the scale factor is 1.','Dilate (2,-3) from origin by factor 2',['Multiply both coordinates by 2.','Image (4,-6).','Distances double.','Angle measures and shape are preserved.'],'(4,-6)','Dilate (-6,4) by factor 1/2 about origin.','(-3,2)','State what changes and what stays invariant.','Dilations create similar figures because proportional lengths change while angle measures remain equal.'),
 L('composition','Compositions of transformations','A composition applies transformations in sequence. Order can matter, so track the image after each step.','Reflect (-4,3) over y-axis, then translate down 5',['Reflection: (-4,3)->(4,3).','Translation: (4,3)->(4,-2).','Final image (4,-2).'],'(4,-2)','Translate (1,2) right 3, then rotate 180° about origin.','(-4,-2)','Record the intermediate point so the order is visible.','Transformation composition is function composition on geometric points; changing order can change the result.'),
 L('properties','Rigid versus non-rigid transformations','Translations, rotations, and reflections are rigid: they preserve distance and angle. Dilations preserve angle but scale distance.','Scale factor 3 dilation',['Every length is multiplied by 3.','Angle measures stay equal.','Shape is similar, not congruent unless scale factor magnitude is 1.'],'angles preserved; lengths tripled','Which properties are preserved by a reflection?','lengths, angle measures, shape; orientation reverses','Compare at least one invariant and one changed property.','Classifying transformations by invariants explains why they prove congruence or similarity.')
